      "paragraphGroups": [
        {
          "blockIndex": 5,
          "source_refs": [
            "p0113#3",
            "p0113#6"
          ],
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "index": 9,
              "sentences": 2,
              "characters": 150,
              "text": "An **ideal plane wave** does not undergo geometric spreading.\nA large thin plate oscillating perpendicular to its surface can approximate such a wave."
            },
            {
              "index": 10,
              "sentences": 3,
              "characters": 174,
              "text": "Assume a uniform, isotropic elastic medium and uniform driving.\nPoints touching the plate then oscillate with equal amplitude and phase.\nThe wave travels normal to the plate."
            },
            {
              "index": 11,
              "sentences": 3,
              "characters": 159,
              "text": "Points on each plane parallel to the plate have the same phase.\nAn equal-phase surface is called a **wavefront**.\nPlane-wave wavefronts are planes (Figure 73)."
            },
            {
              "index": 12,
              "sentences": 2,
              "characters": 111,
              "text": "A finite plate gives only an approximation away from its edges.\nNear an edge, diffraction bends the wavefronts."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockIndex": 17,
          "source_refs": [
            "p0114#8"
          ],
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "index": 24,
              "sentences": 3,
              "characters": 177,
              "text": "In a **transverse wave**, particle displacement is perpendicular to propagation.\nA transverse bulk wave in a solid involves **shear deformation**.\nLayers shift past one another."
            },
            {
              "index": 25,
              "sentences": 3,
              "characters": 170,
              "text": "In the small, pure-shear model, there is no volume change to first order.\nIn an elastic solid, shear produces restoring forces.\nThese forces drive the local oscillations."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
